reasoning_details.text:
Hmm, no tail loss. Why? Because cmd.Wait() — let me reconsider Go's actual behavior. When StdoutPipe is used:
- c.Stdout = pw (an *os.File). In exec.Cmd.Start, since c.Stdout is an *os.File... wait no. StdoutPipe stores: pr, pw := os.Pipe(); c.Stdout = pw; c.parentIOPipes = append(c.parentIOPipes, pr).

In Start(), writerDescriptor(pw): pw IS an *os.File → passed directly as child fd; NO copy goroutine.

In Wait(): 
```go
var err error
if c.ProcessState == nil ... 
state, err := c.Process.Wait()
...
// Wait for the pipe-copying goroutines to complete.
if err := c.watchCtx...; 
c.closeDescriptors(c.parentIOPipes)
```
Hmm, actually the order in modern Go (1.20+): Wait waits for process, then waits for c.goroutine ([...]the copying goroutines), THEN closes parentIOPipes. But with StdoutPipe there are NO copying goroutines (c.goroutine is nil) since fds pass through directly. So close happens right after reap.

But my test shows full capture even with 20ms-per-chunk Progress (1MiB = 32 chunks × 20ms = ~640ms drain) while the child exits in milliseconds and Wait closes the fd immediately. How did the drain read all 1MiB if the fd was closed after ~10ms?

OH WAIT. I need to recheck: does Wait actually close parentIOPipes when StdoutPipe was used? Reading Go source (os/exec/exec.go, go1.22):

```go
func (c *Cmd) Wait() error {
	...
	state, err := c.Process.Wait()
	if err == nil && !state.Success() {
		err = &ExitError{ProcessState: state}
	}
	c.ProcessState = state
	var timer *time.Timer
	if c.watchCtx != nil { ... }
	// Conditions for returning here:
	// ... none
	if c.goroutine != nil {
		// Close the descriptors but don't wait for the goroutines
		...
	}
	wait := func() {...}
	if c.goroutineErr != nil { ... }
	// After invoking the ctx cancellation and waiting for goroutines:
	c.closeDescriptors(c.parentIOPipes)
	...
}
```

Hmm, I genuinely don't remember exact order across versions. Let me check the installed Go version's exec.go source directly. Actually the empirical result says: NO data lost even though child exited ~600ms before drain finished. Two possible explanations:

(a) Wait does NOT close the pipes until... hmm but then what about "Wait will close the pipe after seeing the command exit"?

(b) The reads still succeed after close because... poll.FD.Close unblocks pending ops but subsequent Read on closed FD returns ErrFileClosed — that would ERROR, not deliver buffered data.

The empirical evidence: full 1MiB captured with slow drain. So either close didn't happen early, or closed-fd reads still drained buffered data (impossible per above).

Let me look at the actual Go version used here and its exec.go implementation of StdoutPipe/Wait.

